A||N||8||DaveL||Service Eng Soon Light blues||||8||
Z||000000||DaveL||01-11-2000||03:45 PM||||HELP ! I've put $700 in a $900 car and still won't run right. Ran great when I first bought it even though serv eng soon light came on when ever I got over partial throttle, but it ran great.(87 SE 2.5 DIS) Suddenly one day, it died at a light. Cranked fine, but acted like no fuel. Putting the pedal on the floor got it to start, but it was acting like no fuel. Replaced the fuel filter, no luck. Still died at odd times, and wouldn't restart unless the pedal was floored, even then it seemed like no fuel, but when it would start, there was plenty of fuel odor. Mechanic replaced the ignition module, coils assembly and the crank sensor, and the ECM. Was ok for two days, but same problem returned. Mechanic said there was a short he missed and replaced the ECM and PROM. Now it runs ok most of the time, but the eng light comes on and it loses all its go. Even with it floored, it will only maintain speed. The light flickers on and off. This "mechanic" says it is a loose plug of some sort with 50 some pins in it. Am I being taken for a ride? Can there be false error codes? I'm afraid to take it to GM, but if I want to keep it, I don't see any other way. Did I mention that during my divorce, my ex-wife got in it and tried to destroy the interior? I can forgive anytjhing but that. HELP! ON MY LAST LEGS!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||01-11-2000||04:22 PM||||He could very well be right. It does sound like the ECM is suddenly loosing all its input from the sensors. It's easy to check yourself if there's a loose connector. You need to remove the center console first to get to the ECM.<P>Remove the shifter knob. If it's an automatic, there's a small staple-like piece (don't know what it's called in english) at the back just below the knob. You can remove this with a small screwdriver. Once removed, you can lift the knob and take it off. Next, remove the ashtrays. You will see two screws on both sides. Remove these. Now you will be able to lift the center piece off. This will reveal two more screws from the back part of the center console. Remove. Open the tray between the seats and you'll see two more screws. After you have removed these you should be able to remove the remaining part of the center console. This will reveal the ECM. Don't open it but check for loose plugs. Reseating them a few times might help. Perhaps you could drive the car with the center console removed. If the problem appears, wiggle with the connectors a bit to see if the problem disappears.<P>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaveL||01-12-2000||08:21 AM||||Thanks, I'll try this. I have a Haynes manual which isn't too good. Nothing on my vehicle matches the book. My TBI is different, my throttle cable and transmission cable setup is different, fuel lines are different, and it has a dual exhaust. According to the VIN, it was shipped with a 4 banger. Did Gm ever put dual exhaust on a 4? Well, back to work for awhile.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Raydar||01-12-2000||06:20 PM||||DaveL... The only 4 cyl cars that came with duals were the 84 Indy, and the 86 & 87 SE. <BR>The SE also came with the V-6. All had the GT lower bodywork. Sorry, can't help with your Service Engine light. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||01-13-2000||04:40 PM||||Having to floorboard it to get it started accompanied by fuel odor when it does start would probably mean it is flooded (too much fuel).<P>It is possible to get false error codes.<P>I believe 87 and 88 L4s had a different TBI than 84-86. The illustrations are probably the 84-86. Look toward the back of the book and see if they have a section for updated/newer models with different pictures of the TBI.<P>What codes is it setting?<P><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 01-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||01-13-2000||07:27 PM||||<A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/ogre/pf4cyl1.htm" TARGET=_blank>Read This.</A> It lists many of the variations in the 4 cyl's.<P>You will need either the GM shop book by Helms or the Haynes Fuel Injection book that covers 86-92 to have relevant data for an 87 TBI unit. The Helms book is best but expensive. The Helms book covers DIS ignition etc as well.<P>In short, the whole 4 cyl is worked over in 87 and 88. The new 700 TBI is allot easier to work on than the old 300. DIS has no moving parts.<P>It's also about a requirement from 87 on to have an ECM scanner. There are no manual adjustments to the 87+ motors at all except for static idle. ((Never adjust static idle without a very good reason!))<P>Also check the large connectors by the battery, and every ground wire you can find. There are about a dozen ground wires connected to the head, block, and bell housing. If even on of those is damaged you can have queer problems like this.<P>did anyone bother to check fuel pressure? if the pump is bad or the fuel reg is shot that would tell you real quick. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eddie.H||01-13-2000||08:24 PM||||Dave DID you check the o2 sensor it will make your car do crazy things to .Also check for black smoke out the back when you give it some gas when the 02 sensor takes a sh!t on ya it ill blow black smoke .  	EDDIE GOOD LUCK ||||reg||6
Z||000007||DaveL||01-14-2000||03:21 PM||||Did I mention I had the exhaust manifold replaced, and a new O2 sensor added as part of the original explosion. I really like the car and want to fully restore it(Mid life Crisis?) I have the interior done, then will need paint, and AC repaired. It keeps flashing code 14 and 15, both related to temp sensor circuit. Have replaced this twice, to no avail. Once when it was running without the "serv eng soon" light on, I unplugged the coolant temp sensor, and no light? Spooky. Also realized that some one disconnected the original temp sender in the block and put in an aftermarket sender and guage that reads in degrees. The original wiring harness and plug connector for the sender is just hanging there. Meanwhile the fan is hotwired to run all the time. When I start the engine, the dash guage pegs right and stays. In the front engine compartment on the driver side wheel well in front of the headlight ass., there is a relay which is wired into a ground coming off the wheel well. With this ground disconnected, the fan never runs. I drove it for months with no fan and never had a problem. I bet this ran hot, and the guage malfunctioned. The original owner probably hot wired the fan and put in the aftermarket guage. What an idiot! I either love a challenge or I'm insane.||||reg||1
Z||000008||DRH||01-14-2000||04:12 PM||||DaveL,<P>Codes 14 and 15 are opposite problems with the same sensor. The ECMs temp sensor is located in the thermostat housing. It varies it's resistance to ground with temp change (see chart at site below). One of those codes means the measured resistance is out of range in one direction (open circuit) and the other means it is out in the other direction (short to ground). I can't remember which is which.<P>If you have replaced the ECM sensor with the correct replacement sensor, I would suspect a problem with the wiring. You will need a volt/ohm meter, the Haynes wiring diagram and some basic electrical knowledge to diagnose. The ECM temp sensor, the dash gauge and the cooling fan sensor are SUPPOSED to be 3 independent circuits. With your problems in the other 2 areas the first thing I would check is to see if the previous "electrical re-engineer" crosswired something. Also, since most of the ECM's input is measuring resistanace to ground, a bad ground connection (there are a LOT of them) can cause all kinds of wierd ECM problems.<P><BR>Good Luck.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/coolant-sensor.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/coolant-sensor.html</A> ||||reg||1
